<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hammaburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hammaburg was a fortification built in the early 9th century near the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alster">Alster</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> takes its name. Probably founded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> to secure the Frankish frontier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, it became the seat of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a>'s mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> sacked it in 845.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammaburg.jpg" alt="Hammaburg" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Bowyer (1758-1834) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamburg,_R._Bowyer,_1814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
